  "query_preview": "..A slow moving Pacific storm system will begin to\nprogress through the region over the next 48 hours, bringing\ncontinued rainfall and even some high elevation snowfall. Moisture\nfrom the recent tropical cyclone wrapping up in the larger scale\nlow pressure system will help to maintain well above normal\nprecipitation over the region and, with the increased instability\noverhead, will bring continued higher likelihood of showers and\nthunderstorms bringing moderate to heavy rainfall. Throughout the\nremainder of the day, persistent light to moderate rainfall across\nnorthern Utah will bring a minor risk for areal flooding in poor\ndrainage areas and steep rocky terrain (in addition to recent burn\nscars). For southern and central Utah, the risk of flash flooding\ndue to short-lived moderate to heavy rainfall will remain elevated\nif convection is not entirely muted by cloud cover. As is the case\nwith typical flash flood potential in southern Utah, the highest\nrisk will be found in slot canyons, slickrock areas, dry washes,\nand recent burn scars. \n\nThrough the evening and overnight hours the center of the low will\nbegin to creep into the forecast area, introducing much cooler air\nto the beehive state. As this colder air filters into the region,\nsnow levels will begin dropping from around 9,500ft (as of this\nafternoon) down to around 7,500-8,000ft by Tuesday morning.\nAn area of precipitation is expected to be maintained along this\nboundary of colder air (not quite a well defined cold front), and\nwith snow levels dropping as the colder air oozes into the region\nsnowfall is expected to pick up in intensity in the higher\nelevations (as well as dropping to lower elevations within the\nmountains). For seasonal roads that remain open (e.g. Guardsman\nPass and Bald Mountain pass), accumulating snowfall may pose a\nsignificant risk to travelers who are not prepared.\n\nThe center of this area of low pressure will begin to exit the\nregion through the day on Wednesday, allowing for improving\nconditions areawide. That said, there is some relative uncertainty\nrevolving around how quickly this low pressure moves out of the\nregion which will ultimately affect how quickly we warm up and dry\nout. That said, there is generally high confidence in a ridge\nbuilding into the area late in the week and over the weekend,\nbringing an overall pleasant weekend after a wet start to the\nweek.",
